The kernel programs under bpf/, and the headers written for them, are offered
under GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause; the texts are in LICENSES/. The objects
compiled from them and the Go files bpf2go generates from them (internal/bpf/*.o
and internal/bpf/*_bpfe[lb].go) carry the same terms. An object is a separate
program: a Go binary carries it as data and loads it into the kernel, and no GPL
code is linked into a Go binary. The source of every object is in bpf/.

The vendored libbpf headers under bpf/include/libbpf/ keep their upstream
LGPL-2.1 OR BSD-2-Clause choice.

bpf/include/vmlinux.h is generated, not written: bpftool's dump of the BTF type
information of the Linux 6.17.0-41-generic kernel (Ubuntu), as bpf/README.md
records. Its declarations are the Linux kernel's, which is GPL-2.0-only (text in
LICENSES/GPL-2.0-only.txt), so the terms of bpf/ do not cover it. It carries no
header of its own and is exempt from the SPDX check (ADR 0012). The kernel programs
include it at build time, and the compiled objects keep the type information of
the kernel types they use: names and layouts, which a program needs in order to
run against the kernel it is loaded into (ADR 0012).

The dashboard's icons are Lucide icons, under ISC, and MIT for those derived from
Feather (LICENSES/Lucide.txt).

THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt preserves the license texts and attribution notices for
the Go runtime, linked Go modules, vendored libbpf headers and the Lucide icons.

Raw binary releases are accompanied by tego_<version>_licenses.tar.gz, and the
maze archives hold the same notices. The release checksums and provenance cover
those archives too. Distribute the licenses archive with the executable when
passing a binary on to another recipient.
